use std
into garment_follow_check.amni

Imports: os, math, gfx.obj, gfx.cloth and gfx.garment. A standalone program that checks that a draped garment follows an animated body, on the CPU, and prints one line per check, then "follow: ok" when all pass or "follow: FAILED" when any fails. These already exist; never define them here: `obj::load(path) bool`, `obj::V` (f32, three per vertex), `obj::tris_of(name, out)` (out a growable int list), `obj::centroid(name) real, real, real`; `garment::spec()` (a struct with real fields including `unit`, `fine`, `tie` and `carry`), `garment::make(P, I, joints, s) bool` (P read-only f32 slice, I read-only int slice, joints read-only f32 slice), `garment::drape(steps) int` (the cloth number), `garment::bind(P, I) bool`, `garment::pose(c, Q) int` (Q read-only f32 slice), the f32 lists `garment::pos` and `garment::targets`, the real lists `garment::gp` and `garment::gn` (three per garment vertex), the int lists `garment::sp` (part per shell vertex: 0 torso, 1 left arm, 2 right arm) and real list `garment::st` (place along the part in meters per shell vertex), `garment::joint(k) real, real, real`, `garment::arm_len(side) real` (meters); `cloth::step(c, dt, wx, wy, wz)`, `cloth::particle(c, vertex) int`, the real list `cloth::pos`, `cloth::stretch_now(c) real` and `cloth::speed(c) real`. Time with `clock_ns()` (an int of nanoseconds from the os import, called bare: `os::clock_ns` and `os::clock` do not exist). A module bool `failed` starts false; `check(name, ok, detail)` prints "PASS " or "FAIL " then the name, then " (" the detail ")" when the detail is not empty, and sets `failed` when ok is false. Shell units are decimeters: `unit` is 0.1 meters per unit.

Setup: load "hc/test/gfx/fixtures/mh_tights.obj" (on failure check "load body" as false and stop); the shell triangles are `obj::tris_of("helper-tights", ...)`; the joints are the centroids of the groups "joint-pelvis", "joint-spine-2", "joint-spine-1", "joint-neck", "joint-l-shoulder", "joint-l-elbow", "joint-l-hand", "joint-r-shoulder", "joint-r-elbow", "joint-r-hand", three numbers each in that order. P is `obj::V`. The garment uses `garment::spec()` with `fine` 1, then `make`, then `drape(120)` giving the cloth c.

Animation weights, made once by `weights(I)` after the drape, as real lists with one number per shell vertex: `wa` (left upper arm), `wf` (left forearm) and `wr` (right arm). They start at 1 for left arm vertices (`sp` 1), for left arm vertices whose `st` is above L (L the distance in meters from joint 4 to joint 5 times `unit`), and for right arm vertices (`sp` 2) respectively, else 0. Then they are smoothed like a skinning tool's heat weights: build each shell vertex's neighbor list from the triangles (each distinct vertex sharing a triangle, once), and 40 times replace every weight by the mean of its own value and its neighbors' values (a new list each round, from the previous round's values). `posed(s, Q)` writes into the growable f32 list Q (resized to P's length) the shell at clip time s from 0 to 1, starting from P for every vertex: each vertex is rotated about joint 5 around axis (0, 1, 0) by `wf` times 0.8 times s radians, then about joint 4 around axis (0, 0, 1) by `wa` times 1.2 times s, then about joint 7 around axis (1, 0, 0) by `wr` times 0.5 times the sine of 2 pi s, where rot(p, center, axis, angle) is Rodrigues' rotation of point p about the line through center along the unit axis; the joints are the rest joints. Then every vertex is twisted about the vertical line through joint 0 by 0.35 times the sine of 2 pi s times its twist weight: the larger of its `wa` and `wr`, or else sm(y of joint 0, y of joint 2, its y) when larger, with sm(a, b, x) the smoothstep from a to b. Last every vertex moves by (0, 0.3 times the sine of 4 pi s, 10 s) in shell units (1 meter forward and a 3 cm bob).

F1 bind: `garment::bind(P, I)` gives true, timed; `garment::pose(c, P)` gives the garment vertex count; and every target equals `garment::pos` within 1e-3 shell units. The detail is the vertex count and the bind time in milliseconds with one decimal; it also passes only when bind takes under 1000 ms.

The clip: 60 frames; frame k uses s equal to k over 59, calls `posed`, `garment::pose(c, Q)` and `cloth::step(c, 1/30, 0, 0, 0)`, timing pose and step together. After each frame, for every garment vertex v with particle i (`cloth::particle`): its stray is the distance from the particle's position (`cloth::pos` at 3 i) to its target, times `unit`; its depth is the dot product of (particle position minus glue point `gp`) with the normal `gn`; right after F1 (at rest, before the clip) keep each vertex's rest depth and each cloth edge's rest length: the cloth's edges are e from `cloth::cloths[c].e0` for `cloth::cloths[c].ne` edges, with particles `cloth::edges` at 2 e and 2 e plus 1 and sewn length `cloth::edge_len` at e (reals), and the rest length is the larger of the sewn length and the edge's length right after F1, so gathered fabric may unfold to its sewn length but no further; a vertex is inside at a frame when its depth is below the smaller of its rest depth and 0, minus 0.02 shell units (2 mm deeper than the drape left it). Also keep per frame the shell's own largest edge stretch: over every shell triangle edge, its length in Q over its length in P (skip rest lengths below 1e-9). Keep per frame the largest stray, the mean stray, the share of vertices inside, the fabric stretch (the largest, over the counted cloth edges, of the edge's length over its rest length; counted edges skip rest lengths below 0.02 shell units, 2 mm: seam welding slivers), the share of counted edges whose stretch is above 1.35 times the larger of 1 and that frame's shell stretch, the target stretch (the largest, over the counted edges whose two particles both have a garment vertex, of the distance between the `garment::targets` of the first garment vertex of each particle over the rest length: what the body asks of the fabric), `cloth::speed(c)` times `unit`, and whether every particle coordinate is finite. After the last frame set r1 to the plain F5 offset with Q (mean particle position minus the mean of Q's shell-triangle vertices), never subtracting r0 there: F5 subtracts r0 itself.

F2 follows: the largest stray over the clip is at most 0.035 m and the mean stray over the clip below 0.012 m; detail both in millimeters with one decimal. F3 stays outside: the largest per-frame inside share is at most 0.02; detail as a percentage with two decimals. F4 stable: every frame finite, speed below 3 m/s, at every frame the share of counted cloth edges whose stretch is above 1.35 times the larger of 1 and that frame's shell stretch at most 0.001 (one edge in a thousand); the target stretch is information only, not part of the bar; detail the largest share as a percentage with three decimals, the largest fabric stretch, the largest target stretch and the largest shell stretch with three decimals and the largest speed with two decimals. Print, ungated, "cloth stretch " and the largest `cloth::stretch_now(c)` over the clip with three decimals (measured from the mesh before the drape). F5 rides along: the offset r0 is the mean particle position minus the mean of P's shell-triangle vertices (each vertex counted once per triangle use is fine), taken before the clip; the offset r1 is the same with Q, taken after the last frame (store the plain offset in r1, never r1 minus r0); the drift is the length of r1 minus r0, times `unit`, and must be less than 0.03 m; detail in millimeters with one decimal. F6 cost: the mean time per frame of pose plus step is below 10 ms; detail the mean milliseconds with two decimals and the particle count.

`main` runs the setup, F1, `weights(I)`, the clip, F2 to F6, and prints "follow: ok" or "follow: FAILED".
